  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nNortheasterly winds and temperatures largely below 80F north of \nthe Rio Grande basin persist Saturday, while surface high pressure\nover the Rockies fails to filter down into the area and core of \nrebuilding mid-level ridging that would suppress rain chances \nstays off to the west. This will mean continued daytime \nshower/storm chances each day this weekend, although highest \nchances (25% to 35%) are currently indicated south of the Pecos \nRiver and from the Marfa Plateau into Terrell County. Most likely \nrainfall amounts as well as exact timing and coverage of any \nshowers/storms remain uncertain this far out, but no flash \nflooding threat is indicated at this time. Highs warm back into \nthe 70s and 80s F Sunday and Monday, then solidly into the mid to \nupper 80s F for lowlands and back into the mid to upper 70s F for \nhigher elevations Tuesday as east/northeast winds veer back to \nsoutheasterly. By mid-week next week, 90s F are even indicated for\nthe usual warmer spots along the Pecos River and northeast \nPermian Basin. However, lows will stay in the 50s and 60s F rather\nthan remaining above 70F, while dew point temperatures also \nremain below the mid 50s F for most of the area. Therefore, even \nthe return of warmer weather next week is not yet expected to meet\nor exceed the level of warmth seen earlier last month.",
